The pipeline shape is unchanged — every edit is a path constant, a prose
string, a test fixture, or a JSDoc annotation. No new helpers, no new files,
no signature changes. The orchestrator order stays:

1. Scaffold `.aide/config/brain.aide` (only when `vaultPath` is supplied
   and the file is absent on disk).
2. Compute MCP entries via `writeMcpEntry/index.ts` (which now reads the
   file from the new path).
3. Run framework detection + planning helpers.
4. Apply only `would-create` planning steps.
5. Emit per-file log + warning block.

## Prerequisites

The following modules — already aligned at the spec layer per
`.aide/handoff.aide` — must complete their own `plan.aide` + build cycles
BEFORE this plan's steps execute. The cli/init orchestrator imports their
exports; their internal pipeline changes ripple through this CLI.

- `src/types/index.ts` must export the collapsed `BrainAideConfig`
  (`{ name: string; mcpServerConfig: { command: string; args: string[] } }`)
  and the four-variant `BrainState` union (`ok | no-brain-aide |
  no-mcp-entry | mcp-drift`). Without these the writeMcpEntry wrapper
  cannot derive the brain entry from the new schema.
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts` must export
  `parseBrainAideFromString` validating the two-field schema (rejecting
  `connector` / `rootPath` / `entryFile` / `tools` as
  `malformed-frontmatter`) and `interpolateArgs` generalized over
  frontmatter top-level keys (so the default scaffold's no-placeholder
  args pass through untouched).
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts`
  must emit the `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` launcher with the two-field
  frontmatter shape (`name: obsidian` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`),
  with the supplied vault path inlined byte-for-byte in args. The
  function signature stays `(vaultPath: string) => string`; the
  parameter name may change from `rootPath` to `vaultPath` in that
  module — this plan refers to it as `vaultPath` throughout to match
  the CLI's internal naming.
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts` must scaffold at
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` (not `.aide/brain.aide`). This plan honors
  the same path for the CLI's own scaffold call.
- `src/cli/shared/writeMcpEntry/index.ts` is unchanged in contract by
  this revision — the shared helper's signature (`projectRoot, entries`)
  and behavior (read-parse-merge-write, throw on malformed JSON, no-op
  on already-in-sync) carry forward as-is. This plan documents it as a
  prerequisite only because the init wrapper continues to delegate to it.

If any prerequisite has not landed, this plan's build phase will fail at
typecheck (new `BrainAideConfig` shape) or at runtime (template still emits
old fields). The orchestrator is responsible for sequencing prerequisite
plans first.

## Plan

### 1. Move the brain.aide host path constant in `runInit`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

- [x] Edit `src/cli/init/index.ts`. Two coupled changes:
      - 1a. Replace the `join(cwd, ".aide", "brain.aide")` literal in
        `runInit`'s brain-scaffold step (currently around line 107) with
        `join(cwd, ".aide", "config", "brain.aide")`. Use a single named
        local constant at the top of the function (e.g. `brainAidePath`)
        so the path appears once in the function body. The dirname of
        this path is `.aide/config/`, which is the user-owned ownership
        boundary; the existing `mkdir(dirname(brainAidePath), { recursive:
        true })` call already creates the parent directory tree, so no
        explicit `.aide/config/` `mkdir` step is needed.
      - 1b. Update the per-file log line emitted by the `else`-branch of
        the scaffold check so `displayPath` is `.aide/config/brain.aide`
        (it derives from the path constant; verify the assignment after
        the path change). The `[exists]` branch's `displayPath` likewise
        flips to `.aide/config/brain.aide`. The `message` strings
        (`"Brain config (Obsidian default)"` and `"already present"`) stay
        verbatim — they describe what the file IS, not where it lives.

      Verify after the edit: a grep across `src/cli/init/index.ts` for
      the literal string `".aide/brain.aide"` returns zero hits.

      Tier-3 inline comments at the top of the brain-scaffold block
      narrate the new boundary: "Files inside `.aide/config/` are
      user-owned per the root spec — this scaffold runs once on first
      cold start where the file is absent and never touches the file
      again on any subsequent run, regardless of byte drift from the
      canonical default."

### 2. Mirror the path move in the `writeMcpEntry/` init wrapper

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] Edit `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.ts`. The wrapper reads
      brain.aide from disk (when present) to derive the brain MCP entry;
      the read path must move from `.aide/brain.aide` to
      `.aide/config/brain.aide`. Coupled changes:
      - 2a. In the `if (vaultPath !== undefined)` branch, replace
        `join(projectRoot, ".aide", "brain.aide")` with
        `join(projectRoot, ".aide", "config", "brain.aide")`. Use a
        single local constant for the path so the literal appears once.
      - 2b. Update the error thrown when `parseBrainAideFromString`
        returns a non-`ok` kind so the message names the new path:
        `` `.aide/config/brain.aide could not be parsed: ${reason}. Fix the file and re-run.` ``
        Both the legacy "missing" sub-case and the validation-failure
        sub-case route through this same throw, mirroring today's
        behavior.
      - 2c. Update the JSDoc comment block. The pipeline-step description
        references "If `.aide/brain.aide` exists on disk, reads it" —
        flip that to "If `.aide/config/brain.aide` exists on disk, reads
        it". Add a sentence noting the file lives inside the user-owned
        `.aide/config/` directory and that the wrapper never writes to
        that path; only the orchestrator's seed-semantic scaffold step
        (Step 1) writes it.

      The wrapper's contract with the shared `cli/shared/writeMcpEntry`
      helper is unchanged: still passes
      `{ aide: aideEntry, brain: brainEntry, obsidian: "delete" }` when
      `vaultPath` is supplied; still passes `{ aide: aideEntry }` alone
      when it isn't. The shared helper handles the merge + delete
      semantics; this wrapper only changes WHERE it reads the brain.aide
      bytes from.

      The `WriteMcpEntryResult.message` composition is unchanged: the
      managed-keys set (`aide`, `aidemd-mcp`, `brain`, `obsidian`) and
      the "merged with N existing server(s)" suffix logic stay as-is.

### 3. Collapse deferred-categories into a single Brain-wiring entry

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] Edit `src/cli/init/index.ts`'s `deferredCategories(vaultPath)`
      function. Today it returns three strings when `vaultPath` is absent
      — one for `Brain config`, one for `Brain MCP entry`, one for
      `IDE configuration`. The aligned spec collapses brain config + MCP
      entry into ONE combined "Brain wiring" item because both halves
      descend from the same user-owned `.aide/config/brain.aide` decision.

      New return shape when `vaultPath` is absent (TWO strings, in this
      order):
      ```
      [
        "Brain wiring (.aide/config/brain.aide + derived brain MCP entry) — open Claude Code and run /aide; the orchestrator will prompt for the vault path, scaffold .aide/config/brain.aide, and tell you to run npx aidemd-mcp sync",
        "IDE configuration — re-run: npx aidemd-mcp init --ide <choice>",
      ]
      ```

      When `vaultPath` IS supplied, the return shape is unchanged
      (single IDE entry):
      ```
      [
        "IDE configuration — re-run: npx aidemd-mcp init --ide <choice>",
      ]
      ```

      Update the function's JSDoc to reflect the new vocabulary:
      - replace "Brain config and brain MCP entry (absent when ...)"
        framing with "Brain wiring (combined brain.aide + derived MCP
        entry, absent when ...)";
      - reference `.aide/config/brain.aide` in the explanatory prose;
      - keep the IDE description verbatim;
      - keep the "single source of truth: passed as data to renderWarning"
        framing — that decision is unchanged.

      The combined string is verbatim user-facing copy. It must trace
      EXACTLY to the spec's good example block (the
      `Deferred (each item names how to finish it):` entry that names
      brain wiring as one item). Treat the string as a stable contract
      — tests assert against it byte-for-byte.

