        the helper calls `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` then
        `parseBrainAideFromString` then `interpolateArgs`. After the
        prerequisite plans land, the template emits the new schema and
        the parser validates it; the helper transparently produces the
        new-shape entry. The brain entry's `args` will contain
        `["-y", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", vaultPath]` (POSIX) or
        `["/c", "npx", "-y", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", vaultPath]` (win32).
        Verify the final assertions match the new shape after the
        prerequisite template rewrite.
      - 8d. The "throws on malformed JSON" test stays valid — the shared
        `cli/shared/writeMcpEntry` helper still throws the same error
        message; this wrapper still bubbles it up unchanged.
      - 8e. Add a regression test: when called with `vaultPath` supplied
        and a pre-existing `.aide/config/brain.aide` whose frontmatter
        contains a deprecated field (e.g. `connector: obsidian` left
        from a pre-migration install), `writeMcpEntry` throws via the
        `parseBrainAideFromString` `malformed-frontmatter` branch with
        an error message naming `.aide/config/brain.aide` and the
        deprecated field. This is the regression guard for the parent
        spec's "rejects deprecated fields" invariant flowing through
        the CLI.

### 9. Final cross-file verification

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [x] Run two verification commands and resolve any failures by
      narrowing back to the steps above:
      - 9a. `rtk grep -nF ".aide/brain.aide" src/cli/init/` — must
        return zero hits. Any remaining literal is a missed path
        update from steps 1, 2, 5, 6, 7, or 8.
      - 9b. `rtk grep -nFi "obsidian-mcp" src/cli/init/` — must return
        zero hits in source files (test fixtures referencing the
        prior-cycle launcher are gone after step 8b's audit; help text
        after step 7 names only `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`).
      - 9c. `rtk npm test -- cli/init` — every test file under
        `src/cli/init/` passes against the prerequisite-aligned
        codebase. Failures here either indicate (i) a missed string
        update in this plan's scope, or (ii) a prerequisite plan that
        hasn't landed; the fix is to either complete the missed update
        OR escalate the prerequisite gap back to the orchestrator (do
        NOT shim the CLI to compensate for a missing prerequisite).
      - 9d. `rtk npx tsc --noEmit` — clean typecheck against the new
        `BrainAideConfig` shape and the new `BrainState` four-variant
        union. Any typecheck error in cli/init scope traces back to a
        path constant or an inferred-from-prerequisite-shape mismatch
        the steps above did not catch; resolve by adjusting the local
        edit, never by widening prerequisite types.

      A non-zero exit on any of these commands is a hard halt — do not
      mark the plan complete. Capture the failing output and return it
      to the orchestrator.

## Decisions

- **Brain wiring becomes ONE deferred item, not two.** The aligned spec
  treats `.aide/config/brain.aide` and the derived MCP entry as two
  halves of one user-owned decision: the user owns the file, sync derives
  the entry. Splitting them in the warning duplicates the contract
  surface and forces the user to mentally re-couple two items the spec
  has explicitly fused. The combined string preserves both halves in a
  single line ("brain.aide + derived brain MCP entry") so the user still
  sees what gets installed when they finish the wiring, but treats the
  followup as one decision routed through one surface.
- **Path constants live ONCE per file.** Each edited file (orchestrator,
  init wrapper, two test files) declares the brain.aide host path as a
  single named local constant and references it from every other line.
  The literal string `.aide/config/brain.aide` should appear at most
  once per file as a definition; subsequent uses go through the constant.
  This kills the regression class where one of N call sites gets a path
  update and the others don't.
- **The `.aide/config/` parent directory is the ownership signal — no
  per-file allowlist.** The orchestrator's existing `mkdir(dirname,
  { recursive: true })` call creates `.aide/config/` if absent on the
  first scaffold; subsequent runs find the file present and skip. The
  cli/init code does not maintain a list of "user-owned filenames" — the
  invariant flows from the directory path itself. Step 5e's regression
  test enforces this at the CLI boundary: any file inside `.aide/config/`
  that the CLI finds present is hands-off, regardless of how it got
  there (sync wrote it, the user hand-edited it, a prior init scaffolded
  it). This matches the root spec's "directory path is the ownership
  signal" framing.
- **`writeMcpEntry/` stays a thin wrapper, not absorbed into the shared
  helper.** Today the wrapper composes the entries map (aide entry from
  `mcpEntry()`, brain entry derived from on-disk brain.aide via the
  parser, legacy obsidian deletion) and maps the shared helper's result
  to the CLI's `WriteMcpEntryResult` shape. The aligned spec does not
  change either responsibility; the wrapper continues to live as the
  CLI-specific composition layer. Plan 7 (cli/sync) already extracted
  the read-parse-merge-write primitive into `cli/shared/`; this wrapper
  is the right home for "I am the cold-start CLI with `--vault-path`
  semantics, here are the entries you should write" knowledge.
- **No new helpers, no new files.** The structural refactor lifting
  shared logic into `cli/shared/` already happened in Plan 7. This plan
  is a path-and-prose alignment, not an architectural change. Adding
  new helpers here would be scope creep against a spec that explicitly
  did not change the orchestrator's pipeline shape.
- **Prerequisite plans drive shape changes; this plan only follows.**
  The brain.aide schema collapse (`name` + `mcpServerConfig` only) and
  the boot reporter's four-state shape are owned by `parseBrainAide`,
  `provisionBrain`, and `buildBrainState` plans respectively. This plan
  consumes the new shapes through their existing exports — it never
  reshapes types, never duplicates validation logic, never short-circuits
  the parser. If a prerequisite plan reshapes a type the CLI consumes in
  a way the steps above didn't anticipate, the response is to escalate
  back to the orchestrator and re-plan, not to absorb the change locally.
- **Legacy "Vault path" / "Brain vault scaffolding" guards stay.** The
  prior-cycle test guards (5c) that assert the deferred-categories
  array does NOT contain those legacy strings remain in place as a
  retro-regression guard. The new cycle adds a parallel guard against
  the just-retired "Brain config" / "Brain MCP entry" split. Stacking
  these guards keeps the test file as a record of every vocabulary
  shift the CLI has navigated; deleting the prior-cycle guards would
  let the same string drift back in silently if a future revert happened.
- **Verification is a step, not a side-task.** Step 9's grep + test +
  typecheck commands are an explicit step because path/prose
  refactors are exactly the class of change where one missed reference
  yields a green test suite and a broken cold-start install. Treating
  the verification commands as plan steps means a fresh implementor
  agent runs them and reports results to the orchestrator; the
  orchestrator decides whether to mark the plan complete or escalate.


